# the argument of locke-labour.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# Locke's labour argument; one man and one thing he removes from the common state.
# "property" in S1 (property in his person) and S3 (property in a thing) is kept as separate atoms.
atom	earth_common	the earth and all inferior creatures are common to all men
atom	owns_person	every man has a property in his own person, to which nobody has any right but himself
atom	owns_labour	the labour of his body and the work of his hands are properly his
atom	removes	he removes the thing out of the state that nature hath provided and left it in
atom	mixes	he hath mixed his labour with the thing and joined to it something that is his own
atom	excludes	the labour annexed to the thing excludes the common right of other men to it
atom	enough_left	there is enough, and as good, left in common for others
atom	property	he makes the thing his property
A2	-	removes
P1	S1	earth_common ∧ owns_person	kind normative
P2	S2	owns_labour	kind normative
P3	S3	removes → mixes
P4	S4	owns_labour ∧ mixes ∧ enough_left → excludes	kind normative
# S3 asserts the conclusion without the proviso that S4 adds; the step to it is left as the text gives it.
# "excludes the common right of other men" is plainly taken to amount to his having the thing as his property.
A1	-	excludes → property
C	S3	removes → property	from P2 P3 P4 A1 A2
